Hemorrhagic stroke
Neurological & Neurodegenerative
Thunderclap severe headache, Rapidly declining consciousness, Hyperdense acute blood on CT

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: NRD-016
Reason for Visit: Hyperacute onset of maximum-intensity headache with explosive vomiting and rapidly deteriorating consciousness.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented a witnessed sudden, explosive "thunderclap" headache, followed by unremitting vomiting and progressive lethargy occurring over the last 30 minutes. Immediate in-person assessment is mandated to rule out an acute intracranial hemorrhage, stabilize the patient, and facilitate immediate emergency medical transport prior to the next in-hours period to prevent fatal herniation.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head exploded and I can't see straight." (Spouse: "He screamed, grabbed his head, vomited everywhere, and now he won't wake up properly.")
History of Present Illness: The patient experienced a hyperacute, sudden onset of a severe, explosive, "thunderclap" headache roughly 45 minutes prior to the clinical evaluation, described as completely unlike any previous headache. This was immediately followed by severe, spontaneous nausea and explosive vomiting directly reflecting rapidly rising intracranial pressure. Over the subsequent 30 minutes, there has been a rapid, unyielding deterioration in the level of consciousness; the patient progressed from severe agitation to lethargy and is now obtunded. The spouse notes a brief period of right-sided limb twitching immediately after the headache began, suggestive of focal seizures occurring at disease onset due to direct, acute cortical irritation. The patient exhibits meningismus presenting as severe photophobia and resistance to neck movement. Abnormal, deep respiratory patterns indicative of severe brainstem compression are developing.
Functional Status: Obtunded; completely incapacitated.
Social/Safety Concerns: Immediate life-threatening emergency; inability to protect airway.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 210/115 mmHg | HR: 54 bpm | Temp: 36.9 C | RR: 10 bpm (deep/irregular) | SpO2: 94% | Wt: 85 Kg
General: Obtunded, actively vomiting into a basin, exhibiting Cushing's reflex (hypertension and bradycardia).
Physical Exam: Neurological examination demonstrates a rapidly declining consciousness (GCS 9: E2, V2, M5). Abrupt onset of dense, focal neurological deficits directly corresponding to the exact territory of the bleeding is noted, specifically a dense right-sided hemiparesis with an upgoing plantar response on the right. Pupils are unequal; left pupil is 5mm and sluggish, right is 3mm and brisk. Fundoscopic exam is deferred due to urgency and lack of mydriatic drops, but severe intracranial hypertension is clinically evident. Diagnostic tetrad elements are overwhelmingly present, specifically the thunderclap severe headache and rapidly declining consciousness.
Environmental Observations: Soiled clothing and bedding from emesis.
Medication Reconciliation: Apixaban 5mg BD for atrial fibrillation (last dose taken this morning).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hemorrhagic Stroke (Intracerebral Hemorrhage with suspected uncal herniation) - Status: Critical Exacerbation
Differentials:

Acute Migraine Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Explosive onset to maximum intensity instantly, presence of severe hypertension, focal neurological deficits (hemiparesis), and obtundation.

Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Hyperacute (seconds to minutes) onset rather than hours to days, afebrile presentation, and presence of profound focal motor deficits.

Seizure with Todd's Paresis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the "thunderclap" headache preceding the seizure, profound hypertension, and signs of escalating intracranial pressure (Cushing's reflex).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Extreme

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the classic, catastrophic presentation of an acute hemorrhagic stroke in the setting of therapeutic anticoagulation. The explosive onset of the thunderclap headache, coupled with explosive vomiting, profound malignant hypertension, bradycardia, and a rapidly declining GCS, unequivocally signals massive intracranial hemorrhage with impending tentorial herniation. Delaying assessment or intervention by even minutes is lethal. Urgent after-hours deployment was critical to secure the airway, recognize the lethal triad of intracranial hypertension, and coordinate an immediate, high-priority emergency ambulance transfer for neurosurgical intervention and urgent anticoagulant reversal.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Activated Emergency Medical Services (000) for immediate Priority 1 transfer. Placed patient in a 30-degree head-up position. Secured peripheral IV access. Prepared detailed clinical handover for the receiving emergency physician and neurosurgical team.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Step 1: By strictly maintaining the head of the bed elevated at 30 degrees while awaiting the ambulance, the caregiver actively promotes cerebral venous drainage, immediately mitigating the spike in intracranial pressure.
Step 2: By placing the patient in the lateral decubitus (recovery) position when active vomiting occurs, the caregiver utilizes gravity to clear the oropharynx, preventing catastrophic aspiration pneumonia.
Step 3: By rapidly collecting all current medication boxes (specifically the Apixaban) for the paramedics, the caregiver provides the hospital team with the exact agent requiring immediate chemical reversal upon arrival.
Home-Based Services (Optional): N/A - Immediate Hospitalization.
DME/Safety (Optional): N/A
Follow-up: Tertiary hospital neurosurgical team to manage.

